+# Contract: `logredact` command-line interface
+
+**Branch**: `001-logredact-cli` | **Date**: 2026-11-05 | **Spec**: [../spec.md](../spec.md)
+
+This is the complete external interface of the feature. It is the contract tests' reference: every
+clause here is observable from outside the process (arguments in; stdout bytes, stderr bytes, and
+exit status out) and each one is traceable to a requirement.
+
+## Invocation
+
+```
+python -m logredact [--count] [--] [FILE ...]
+```
+
+Requires `src/` on `PYTHONPATH` and Python 3.11 or newer (A-10).
+
+### Argument grammar (allowlist — SR-001)
+
+| Token | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| `--count` | Write a tally to stderr after all output. May appear at most once. |
+| `--` | End of options. Every later token is a file operand, even one starting with `-`. |
+| anything else not starting with `-` | A file operand. |
+
+Nothing else is accepted. The allowlist is closed, so all of the following are **rejected**:
+`--cou` (no abbreviation), `--count=1`, `-h`, `--help`, `--verbose`, and any other option.
+
+> `-h` / `--help` are rejected deliberately, not by oversight — SR-001 permits only `--count`, and
+> argparse's built-in help would write to stdout and exit 0, which SR-001 forbids. See
+> [research.md R9](../research.md), which flags this as a deviation from CLI convention for the
+> requester to confirm.
+
+### Operands
+
+- Zero operands → read standard input (FR-002).
+- One or more operands → read each, in the order given, and never read standard input (FR-003).
+- Order is honoured, not sorted (Story 2 scenario 2). A repeated operand is read again
+  (Story 2 scenario 4).
+- An operand that cannot be opened or read is reported on stderr and **skipped**; every remaining
+  operand is still processed, and the run exits 2 at the end (FR-012,
+  [research.md R10](../research.md)). Each failing operand produces its own diagnostic, so the
+  diagnostics alone identify which files are missing from the output.
+- Operands are opened read-only, exactly as supplied: no globbing, no path construction, no
+  normalisation (SR-008). Operands are never written, created, or truncated (FR-017).
+
+## Streams
+
+| Stream | Content |
+|---|---|
+| stdin | Read only when there are no operands. |
+| stdout | The redacted input, and nothing else — ever. No diagnostics, no tally. |
+| stderr | Diagnostics and the `--count` tally only. Never any input data (SR-003). |
+
+Standard output is byte-for-byte the input with matches replaced by their placeholders (FR-008,
+FR-009). Line terminators (`\n`, `\r\n`, a lone `\r`, or a missing final terminator) are reproduced
+exactly; none is added or removed. Bytes that are not valid UTF-8 are reproduced unchanged (A-4).
+
+## Exit status
+
+| Status | Meaning | Source |
+|---|---|---|
+| `0` | The whole input was redacted and fully written. | FR-015 |
+| `2` | Run failed: one or more operands could not be opened or read (FR-012), a line exceeded the cap (FR-018), an argument was rejected (SR-001, A-5), or output could not be written (SR-009). |
+| `141` | Standard output was closed early by the consumer (`128 + SIGPIPE`). Not a successful run. | A-7 |
+
+A non-zero status always means **the output on stdout is incomplete and must not be treated as a
+complete redaction**. Output already written before a failure stays written (FR-012, FR-018).
+Status 2 is a single failure status however many operands failed: it is not a count, and there is
+no distinct status per failure kind (A-5).
+
+### Whether the run continues after a failure
+
+| Failure | Remaining operands | When the status is decided |
+|---|---|---|
+| Operand cannot be opened or read | **processed**; each failure gets its own diagnostic | 2, at the end of the run |
+| Line exceeds the 1 MiB cap | **not** processed; the run stops immediately | 2, at once |
+| Rejected argument | nothing is read or written at all | 2, before any I/O |
+| Output write/flush failure | run ends; continuing cannot help | 2, at once |
+
+The first two differ deliberately: FR-018 states that an over-length line "MUST stop processing",
+while FR-012 leaves continuation open and the requester directed `cat`-style behaviour for it
+([research.md R10](../research.md)).
+
+## Output formats
+
+### Placeholders
+
+| Kind | Replacement |
+|---|---|
+| email address | `[EMAIL]` |
+| IPv4 address | `[IP]` |
+| payment card number | `[CARD]` |
+
+Fixed literals. Not configurable (A-9). A match is replaced in full, leaving no characters or
+length information from the original (SR-006).
+
+### `--count` tally
+
+Exactly one line on stderr, written after stdout has been written and flushed (A-3):
+
+```
+emails=N ips=N cards=N
+```
+
+`N` is a decimal count of **replacements made** across all inputs in the run — per occurrence, not
+per distinct value, and not per file (FR-010, A-1, Story 3 scenario 3). The line contains nothing
+else: no sample values, no line numbers, no file names (SR-004).
+
+The tally is written **only** when `--count` is given **and** the run exits 0. No tally is written
+on any failing run, so a partial count cannot be mistaken for a complete one (FR-018, generalised
+in [research.md R10](../research.md)).
+
+Without `--count`, a successful run writes **nothing at all** to stderr (FR-011).
+
+### Diagnostics
+
+One line on stderr, prefixed with the program name, containing no input data and no traceback
+(SR-003). Shapes:
+
+| Situation | Shape | Requirement |
+|---|---|---|
+| Operand cannot be opened/read | `python -m logredact: error: cannot read 'FILE': REASON` — one such line per failing operand, written as it is encountered | FR-012 |
+| Line over the cap | `python -m logredact: error: SOURCE: line exceeds the maximum length of 1048576 characters` | FR-018 |
+| Rejected argument | argparse usage line plus `python -m logredact: error: …` | SR-001 |
+| Output write failure | `python -m logredact: error: cannot write output: REASON` | SR-009 |
+
+`FILE` is the operand exactly as written on the command line (FR-012). `SOURCE` is that same
+operand, or `standard input`. `REASON` is the operating system's message (for example
+`No such file or directory`, `Is a directory`, `Permission denied`) — it names the file and the
+reason, never file contents.
+
+## Behavioural contract
+
+| ID | Given | Then |
+|---|---|---|
+| C-01 | `user jane@example.com from 10.0.0.7\n` on stdin, no operands | stdout exactly `user [EMAIL] from [IP]\n`; stderr empty; status 0 |
+| C-02 | `paid with 4111 1111 1111 1111, ref 1234567890123\n` on stdin | stdout exactly `paid with [CARD], ref 1234567890123\n`; status 0 |
+| C-03 | `host 999.1.1.1 and 192.168.0.10\n` on stdin | stdout exactly `host 999.1.1.1 and [IP]\n`; status 0 |
+| C-04 | `a@b.io 1.2.3.4\n` on stdin, `--count` | stdout exactly `[EMAIL] [IP]\n`; stderr exactly `emails=1 ips=1 cards=0\n`; status 0 |
+| C-05 | empty stdin | stdout empty; stderr empty; status 0 |
+| C-06 | empty stdin, `--count` | stdout empty; stderr exactly `emails=0 ips=0 cards=0\n`; status 0 |
+| C-07 | operands `a.log b.log` | stdout is redacted `a.log` immediately followed by redacted `b.log`; status 0 |
+| C-08 | operands `b.log a.log` | the reverse order of C-07 |
+| C-09 | any operand given | stdin is not read |
+| C-10 | the same operand twice | its redacted contents appear twice |
+| C-11 | operand naming a missing path | stderr names that path; stdout unaffected; status 2 |
+| C-12 | operand naming a directory | stderr names that path; status 2 |
+| C-13 | operand whose permissions deny reading | stderr names that path; status 2 |
+| C-23 | operands `a.log missing.log b.log` | stdout is redacted `a.log` followed by redacted `b.log`; stderr names `missing.log`; status 2 |
+| C-24 | operands `missing1.log missing2.log a.log` | stderr contains one diagnostic naming `missing1.log` and one naming `missing2.log`, in operand order; stdout is redacted `a.log`; status 2 |
+| C-25 | operand with an over-length line followed by a readable operand | the second operand is **not** read and none of it appears on stdout; status 2 (FR-018) |
+| C-26 | unreadable operand with `--count` | no `emails=… ips=… cards=…` line; stderr has only the per-file diagnostics; status 2 |
+| C-14 | any failing run | stderr has no traceback and no input content |
+| C-15 | unknown option `--verbose` | stderr describes the rejected option; stdout empty; status non-zero (2) |
+| C-16 | third line is 1 MiB + 1 characters | first two lines appear redacted on stdout; no part of line 3 on stdout; stderr names the source and reports the length; status 2 |
+| C-17 | a line of exactly 1 MiB including its terminator | that line is redacted and emitted normally; status 0 |
+| C-18 | over-length line with `--count` | no `emails=… ips=… cards=…` line is written; status 2 |
+| C-19 | stdout redirected to a file, `--count` | the tally is not in that file |
+| C-20 | `--` followed by `-weird.log` | `-weird.log` is treated as a file operand |
+| C-21 | input with no redactable values | stdout is byte-for-byte the input |
+| C-22 | stdout closed early (e.g. piped to `head -1`) | no traceback on stderr; status 141 |
+
+C-01 through C-04 are the spec's worked examples (SC-001).
