+# Contract: redaction rules
+
+**Branch**: `001-logredact-cli` | **Date**: 2026-11-05 | **Spec**: [../spec.md](../spec.md)
+
+The recognition contract: what counts as a match, how overlaps are resolved, and the exact
+patterns. This is the reference for the unit tests. Every pattern below was validated in a
+prototype against the 28 cases listed at the end ([research.md R8](../research.md)).
+
+## Precedence and scanning
+
+One compiled pattern, three named alternatives **in precedence order** (FR-014, A-2):
+
+```
+(?P<email>…) | (?P<ip>…) | (?P<card>…)
+```
+
+Applied once per line with `pattern.sub(replacer, line)`. This yields the required semantics
+directly: leftmost match wins, matches never overlap, alternatives are tried in written order at
+each position, and replaced text is never re-scanned (FR-014). Each call of the replacer is one
+counted replacement (A-1).
+
+Email beats IPv4 because every character that can start or continue an IPv4 address is also an
+email local-part character, so an overlapping email match always starts at or before the IPv4
+match and wins on the leftmost rule as well as on precedence (A-2, [research.md R4](../research.md)).
+
+## Email → `[EMAIL]`
+
+```
+(?<![A-Za-z0-9._%+-])[A-Za-z0-9._%+-]+@[A-Za-z0-9-]+(?:\.[A-Za-z0-9-]+)*\.[A-Za-z]{2,}
+```
+
+| Element | Rule | Source |
+|---|---|---|
+| local part | one or more of letters, digits, `.` `_` `%` `+` `-` | FR-004 |
+| `@` | literal, exactly one | FR-004 |
+| domain | dot-separated labels of letters, digits, hyphens | FR-004 |
+| last label | at least two letters, no digits or hyphens | FR-004 |
+| domain label count | **at least two** (at least one dot required) | [R6](../research.md) |
+| leading guard | the local part may not begin mid-token | [R5](../research.md), FR-013 in spirit |
+
+The leading lookbehind is **required for linear-time matching** (SR-005), not cosmetic: without it
+the pattern is quadratic in line length and a 1 MiB line takes hours. It provably does not change
+which text matches ([research.md R5](../research.md)).
+
+| Input | Result |
+|---|---|
+| `jane@example.com` | `[EMAIL]` |
+| `jane.doe+test@mail.example.com` | `[EMAIL]` |
+| `mail jane@example.com.` | `mail [EMAIL].` — the sentence period is not part of the match |
+| `a@1.2.3.4.example.com` | `[EMAIL]` — email precedence, redacted once, counted once |
+| `user@localhost` | unchanged — no dot in the domain ([R6](../research.md)) |
+| `@property` | unchanged — no local part |
+| `jane@example.c` | unchanged — last label shorter than two letters |
+
+## IPv4 address → `[IP]`
+
+```
+(?<![0-9.]) OCTET (?: \. OCTET ){3} (?![0-9.])
+
+OCTET = (?:25[0-5]|2[0-4][0-9]|1[0-9][0-9]|[1-9][0-9]|[0-9])
+```
+
+Four dot-separated decimal octets, each 0–255, with no leading zeros beyond a single `0`
+(FR-005). Neither end may be flanked by a digit or a dot (FR-013). The octet branches are mutually
+exclusive, so there is nothing to backtrack into — linear by construction (SR-005).
+
+| Input | Result |
+|---|---|
+| `0.0.0.0`, `255.255.255.255`, `192.168.0.10` | `[IP]` |
+| `999.1.1.1`, `256.1.1.1` | unchanged — octet out of range |
+| `1.2.3`, `1.2.3.4.5` | unchanged — wrong number of octets, and flanking dots |
+| `01.2.3.4` | unchanged — leading zero; the inner `1.2.3.4` is blocked by the preceding digit |
+| `v10.0.0.7-beta` | `v[IP]-beta` — **redacted**; flanked by `v` and `-`, neither a digit nor a dot ([R7](../research.md)) |
+
+## Payment card number → `[CARD]`
+
+### Candidate shape
+
+The alternative matches a **maximal run** of digit groups joined by single separators:
+
+```
+(?<!\d) \d+ (?: [ -] \d+ )*
+```
+
+Within that run, a *candidate* is a contiguous sequence of whole groups where:
+
+1. the total digit count is 13 to 19 (FR-006);
+2. every separator inside it is the **same** character, a single space or a single hyphen
+   (FR-006);
+3. its digits pass the Luhn checksum (FR-006, FR-007);
+4. it begins at a group's first digit and ends at a group's last digit — which is exactly FR-013's
+   "not flanked by a digit".
+
+Candidates are selected **leftmost first, longest at that position**, replaced by `[CARD]`, and
+scanning continues after the replaced candidate. Every character of the run that is not part of a
+redacted candidate — including separators between two adjacent cards — is reproduced unchanged
+(FR-008). Each replacement counts once (A-1).
+
+Checking only the whole run would leave a real card in the output when it follows other bare
+numbers, so sub-candidates must be considered; the selection runs in linear time with O(1) extra
+memory ([research.md R8](../research.md)).
+
+### Luhn
+
+Right to left over the candidate's digits (separators removed): double every second digit,
+subtracting 9 from any result above 9; the total must be divisible by 10.
+
+| Input | Result | Why |
+|---|---|---|
+| `4111111111111111` | `[CARD]` | 16 digits, unbroken, Luhn-valid |
+| `4111 1111 1111 1111` | `[CARD]` | single spaces throughout |
+| `4111-1111-1111-1111` | `[CARD]` | single hyphens throughout |
+| `1234567890123` | unchanged | 13 digits, Luhn fails (FR-007) |
+| `4111  1111  1111  1111` | unchanged | doubled separators break the run into 4-digit groups |
+| `4111 1111-1111 1111` | unchanged | mixed separators; no uniform candidate reaches 13 digits |
+| `4111--1111--1111--1111` | unchanged | doubled separators |
+| `1234114111111111111111` | unchanged | one 22-digit group; the Luhn-valid 16-digit substring is flanked by digits (FR-013) |
+| `94111 1111 1111 1111` | unchanged | the valid card would start mid-group, flanked by `9` |
+| `4111 1111 1111 11110` | unchanged | the valid card would end mid-group, flanked by `0` |
+| `log 1 2 3 4111 1111 1111 1111 end` | `log 1 2 3 [CARD] end` | sub-candidate inside a longer run |
+| `4111 1111 1111 1111 4111 1111 1111 1111` | `[CARD] [CARD]` | two candidates; the separator between them is preserved |
+| `x4111 1111 1111 1111` | `x[CARD]` | `x` is not a digit, so FR-013 does not block |
+
+## Pass-through guarantees
+
+- Every character not part of a match is written unchanged, in its original position (FR-008).
+- A line with nothing to redact is byte-for-byte identical, terminator included (FR-009, SC-003).
+- Line terminators and invalid UTF-8 bytes are never altered (A-4). Lone surrogates representing
+  undecodable bytes fall outside every character class above and cannot be part of a match.
+- A placeholder in the input (a line that literally contains `[EMAIL]`) is not special and is
+  passed through like any other text.
+
+## Prototype validation
+
+All 28 cases in this contract plus the empty line, a CRLF line, and the four worked examples from
+the spec were verified against a prototype of these rules: **28/28 pass**. Two defects were found
+and fixed during that prototyping, and both carry a required regression test: separators between
+adjacent cards were swallowed (FR-008), and the first candidate-search design was 4.6x slower and
+allocated memory proportional to the run length (SR-005, SC-010). See
+[research.md R8](../research.md).
